{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not covet your fellow's wife.</b> So as to cause him to divorce her and you will marry her. And one should not explain that you seize her from him by force, like 'and they coveted houses and seized' — for he already warned 'you shall not commit adultery.' 'You shall not covet' — both occurrences of לא תחמד are without vav. The five first commandments — לא מבעיא, he says: לא מבעיא that you accept Me as master over you, but even another deity — 'you shall have no other' — and לא מבעיא 'you shall have no other,' but even in an oath — 'you shall not take His name in vain.' And לא מבעיא 'you shall not take His name in vain,' but even Sabbath — since He rested upon it, you must keep it for His name; and לא מבעיא 'remember the Sabbath day,' but even honoring father and mother, which parallels My honor — I warn you. And the five latter ones likewise — לא מבעיא, he says: לא מבעיא 'you shall not murder,' but even adultery I warn you; and לא מבעיא adultery, but even theft I warn you; and לא מבעיא 'you shall not steal,' but even false testimony I warn you; and לא מבעיא 'you shall not testify,' but even coveting I warn you; and לא מבעיא his house, which involves monetary loss, but even his wife, which involves no monetary loss, for if he wishes he marries another without monetary loss — I warn you; and לא מבעיא his wife, but even his servant; and לא מבעיא his servant, whose value is great, but even his donkey; and לא מבעיא his donkey, but even anything that belongs to your fellow. In most of the commandments there are two cantillation signs, to teach that on Shavuot, which is a model of the giving of the Torah, they translate the commandments and read every utterance of 'you shall have no other' and every utterance of 'remember' with the large cantillation signs, to make each one of them a single verse, for each one of them is one utterance by itself. And the commandments 'you shall not murder,' 'you shall not commit adultery,' 'you shall not steal,' 'you shall not testify' they read with the small cantillation signs, to make four verses, for they are four commandments. But in the month of Shevat, when they read in the portion of Yitro as on the other Sabbaths of the year, they read 'you shall have no other' and 'remember' with the small cantillation signs, to make of each one of them four verses, and the commandments 'you shall not murder,' 'you shall not commit adultery,' 'you shall not steal,' 'you shall not testify' they read with the large cantillation signs, to make them one verse — because we do not find in all Scripture a verse of two words besides these, and specifically on Shavuot, as I explained above. Also in the commandments 'I' and 'you shall have no other' there is a large cantillation sign to make them both one verse, in remembrance that they were said in one utterance. How so? On the word אנכי — pashṭa; on the word אלהיך — zaqef qatan; on the word הוצאתיך — ṭlisha; on the word מארץ — qadma ve-azla; on the word מצרים — and on the word עבדים — revi'i."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:12.763973385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}